\title{The Algebraic Core of Minimal Ordinary Modularity Lifting\\for Genus-Two Siegel Systems}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 17, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We isolate the commutative-algebra mechanism underlying minimal ordinary modularity lifting for genus-two Siegel modular forms in the stable-Yoshida setting. Let $\Lambda$ be a weight algebra, $R$ a universal minimal ordinary deformation ring, and $\T$ an ordinary Hecke algebra. Assuming an arithmetic comparison $R\cong\T$ over $\Lambda$, we show that coefficient-valued deformation points and Hecke eigenpackets are naturally and mutually inversely identified; every deformation point consequently has a unique modular realization. Uniqueness already follows from a surjective deformation-to-Hecke presentation. Freeness over $\Lambda$ transfers in both directions, with finite rank preserved. Finally, specialization of the weight algebra at a maximal ideal is an integral domain, indeed a field. Explicit algorithms and finite examples illustrate point transport, basis transport, cancellation, and residual specialization. The results separate the universal algebraic output of an $R=\T$ theorem from the deep arithmetic input required to construct that comparison for stable Yoshida residual representations.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Modularity lifting theorems compare a Galois deformation problem with an automorphic Hecke problem. The former organizes lifts of a residual representation satisfying prescribed local and global conditions; the latter organizes systems of eigenvalues of commuting Hecke operators. Their coordinate rings are traditionally denoted by $R$ and $\T$. An isomorphism $R\cong\T$ says that these constructions describe the same algebraic space.

The motivating setting concerns Siegel modular forms of genus two. Their Galois representations are four-dimensional and symplectic. The residual representation is assumed to arise from a stable Yoshida lift, namely automorphic induction of a nearly ordinary Hilbert modular eigencuspform over a real quadratic field. Minimal ramification limits the allowed deformations, ordinarity controls local behavior at a residue characteristic $p$, and regularity hypotheses place classical weights in a tractable range.

Those hypotheses belong to the proof of the arithmetic comparison. Once the comparison exists, its principal consequences are general commutative algebra. This paper develops that formal core. The main result is a natural bijection, for every coefficient algebra $A$, between $\Lambda$-algebra maps $R\to A$ and $\T\to A$. In arithmetic language, every minimal ordinary deformation point is modular and determines exactly one eigenpacket. The comparison also transports free module structures. A separate cancellation theorem proves uniqueness from a surjection $R\twoheadrightarrow\T$, and maximal-ideal specialization ensures that the residual weight ring has no zero divisors.

The unity of these conclusions is the main organizing observation. Point transport expresses modularity, inverse transport expresses uniqueness, linear transport expresses freeness, and quotient-field structure controls residual weights. All are properties of a common represented object rather than accidents of one presentation.

\section{Algebraic setting}

\subsection{Weight space and coefficient algebras}

Let $\Lambda$ be a commutative ring with identity. In ordinary genus-two applications, it is a two-variable Iwasawa algebra, typically modeled by $\mathcal O[[X_1,X_2]]$ for a $p$-adic valuation ring $\mathcal O$. A commutative $\Lambda$-algebra is a commutative ring equipped with a fixed structure map from $\Lambda$; all homomorphisms below preserve this structure.

Conceptually, let $R$ represent four-dimensional symplectic Galois lifts satisfying ordinary, minimally ramified, polarized deformation conditions. For a commutative $\Lambda$-algebra $A$, an \emph{$A$-valued deformation point} is a map
\[
 \rho:R\longrightarrow A
\]
of $\Lambda$-algebras.

Let $\T$ be the localized ordinary Hecke algebra attached to the relevant genus-two automorphic space. An \emph{$A$-valued Hecke eigenpacket} is a $\Lambda$-algebra map
\[
 \phi:\T\longrightarrow A.
\]
It assigns compatible values to all Hecke operators represented by $\T$.

\begin{definition}[Minimal ordinary comparison datum]
A minimal ordinary comparison datum over $\Lambda$ consists of commutative $\Lambda$-algebras $R$ and $\T$ and a $\Lambda$-algebra isomorphism
\[
 c:R\overset{\sim}{\longrightarrow}\T.
\]
\end{definition}

Producing $c$ is the arithmetic content of an $R=\T$ theorem. We assume it and derive its algebraic output.

\section{Transport of points}

For a commutative $\Lambda$-algebra $A$, write
\[
 \Def_A=\Hom_{\Lambda\text{-alg}}(R,A),\qquad
 \Eig_A=\Hom_{\Lambda\text{-alg}}(\T,A).
\]
Define transport maps
\[
 F_A:\Def_A\to\Eig_A,\quad F_A(\rho)=\rho\circ c^{-1},
\]
and
\[
 G_A:\Eig_A\to\Def_A,\quad G_A(\phi)=\phi\circ c.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Inverse transport on deformation points]
For every $\rho\in\Def_A$, one has $G_A(F_A(\rho))=\rho$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Associativity and $c^{-1}\circ c=\mathrm{id}_R$ give
\[
G_A(F_A(\rho))=(\rho\circ c^{-1})\circ c
=\rho\circ(c^{-1}\circ c)=\rho.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Inverse transport on eigenpackets]
For every $\phi\in\Eig_A$, one has $F_A(G_A(\phi))=\phi$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Similarly,
\[
F_A(G_A(\phi))=(\phi\circ c)\circ c^{-1}
=\phi\circ(c\circ c^{-1})=\phi.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Abstract Minimal Modularity Lifting Theorem]
Let $R$ and $\T$ be commutative $\Lambda$-algebras equipped with a $\Lambda$-algebra isomorphism $c:R\to\T$. For every commutative $\Lambda$-algebra $A$ and every deformation point $\rho:R\to A$, there exists a unique Hecke eigenpacket $\phi:\T\to A$ satisfying
\[
 \phi\circ c=\rho.
\]
Explicitly, $\phi=\rho\circ c^{-1}$. Thus $\Def_A$ and $\Eig_A$ are naturally bijective.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Define $\phi=\rho\circ c^{-1}$. Then $\phi\circ c=\rho$. If $\psi\circ c=\rho$, then
\[
\psi=\psi\circ c\circ c^{-1}=\rho\circ c^{-1}=\phi.
\]
The preceding lemmas establish the mutually inverse bijections.
\end{proof}

The theorem applies not only to field-valued classical points but to valuation rings, Artinian rings carrying infinitesimal data, and auxiliary family algebras.

\begin{proposition}[Naturality in coefficients]
If $f:A\to B$ is a homomorphism of commutative $\Lambda$-algebras, then
\[
f\circ F_A(\rho)=F_B(f\circ\rho),\qquad
f\circ G_A(\phi)=G_B(f\circ\phi).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Both identities are associativity of composition; for instance,
$f\circ(\rho\circ c^{-1})=(f\circ\rho)\circ c^{-1}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Uniqueness from surjectivity}

An isomorphism is required for bidirectional transport, but uniqueness alone requires only a surjective presentation.

\begin{theorem}[Surjective Eigenpacket Uniqueness]
Let $q:R\to\T$ be a surjective homomorphism of commutative $\Lambda$-algebras. If $\phi,\psi:\T\to A$ are $\Lambda$-algebra maps and
\[
 \phi\circ q=\psi\circ q,
\]
then $\phi=\psi$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $t\in\T$, choose $r\in R$ with $q(r)=t$. Then
\[
\phi(t)=\phi(q(r))=(\phi\circ q)(r)
=(\psi\circ q)(r)=\psi(q(r))=\psi(t).
\]
Thus the maps agree everywhere.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why surjectivity matters]
Let $R=k$, $\T=k[x]$, and include constants by $q:k\hookrightarrow k[x]$. Evaluation at $x=0$ and at $x=1$ are distinct maps $k[x]\to k$ agreeing after composition with $q$. Agreement on the visible coordinates does not determine values on a missing generator.
\end{remark}

In arithmetic applications, regularity and local structure may be used to establish the needed surjectivity. Once surjectivity is available, family uniqueness is the cancellation statement above.

\section{Freeness over weight space}

A $\Lambda$-algebra isomorphism is in particular a $\Lambda$-linear equivalence, so it transports bases.

\begin{theorem}[Bidirectional Freeness Transfer]
Let $c:R\to\T$ be an isomorphism of commutative $\Lambda$-algebras. Then $R$ is a free $\Lambda$-module if and only if $\T$ is a free $\Lambda$-module.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $(e_i)_{i\in I}$ is a $\Lambda$-basis of $\T$. We claim $(c^{-1}(e_i))_{i\in I}$ is a basis of $R$. For $r\in R$, expand $c(r)=\sum_i\lambda_i e_i$ and apply $c^{-1}$ to obtain
$r=\sum_i\lambda_i c^{-1}(e_i)$, so the family spans. Applying $c$ to a linear relation among the $c^{-1}(e_i)$ reduces it to a relation among the $e_i$, proving independence. The converse transports a basis through $c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Finite rank preservation]
If either $R$ or $\T$ is finite free of rank $n$ over $\Lambda$, then the other is finite free of rank $n$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Transport a basis containing exactly $n$ elements.
\end{proof}

Geometrically, finite freeness gives a finite flat map to weight space with constant algebraic rank. It does not by itself imply that the map is unramified or generically \'{e}tale.

\subsection{Basis-transport algorithm}

Given an explicit basis $e_1,\dots,e_n$ of $\T$, compute $r_i=c^{-1}(e_i)$ for each $i$ and return $r_1,\dots,r_n$. The theorem proves that this output is a basis of $R$. If evaluating $c^{-1}$ costs $C_c$, the transport costs $O(nC_c)$. In matrix coordinates, precomputing the inverse of an $n\times n$ comparison matrix costs $O(n^3)$ field operations by Gaussian elimination.

\section{Residual weight specialization}

\begin{theorem}[Residual Weight Ring Theorem]
Let $\Lambda$ be a commutative ring with identity and let $\mathfrak m$ be a maximal ideal. Then $\Lambda/\mathfrak m$ is a field and therefore an integral domain.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $[a]\ne0$ in $\Lambda/\mathfrak m$. Then $a\notin\mathfrak m$, so the ideal $\mathfrak m+(a)$ strictly contains $\mathfrak m$. By maximality it equals $\Lambda$. Thus $1=m+ab$ for some $m\in\mathfrak m$ and $b\in\Lambda$. Modulo $\mathfrak m$ this gives $[a][b]=[1]$. Hence every nonzero class is invertible. A field has no zero divisors.
\end{proof}

This theorem concerns the weight residue ring. It does not assert that every deformation or Hecke fiber over that field is reduced or integral; such statements need additional hypotheses.

\section{Computational models}

\begin{example}[Rank-two comparison]
Let
\[
\Lambda=\mathbb Z,\quad
R=\mathbb Z[x]/(x^2-2),\quad
\T=\mathbb Z[y]/(y^2-2).
\]
The map $c(x)=y$ is an isomorphism. Both rings are free of rank $2$, with bases $\{1,x\}$ and $\{1,y\}$. In $A=\mathbb F_7$, both $3$ and $4$ square to $2$. The deformation point $x\mapsto3$ transports uniquely to $y\mapsto3$, while $x\mapsto4$ transports uniquely to $y\mapsto4$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Surjective cancellation]
Let $R=\mathbb Z/12\mathbb Z$, $\T=\mathbb Z/6\mathbb Z$, and let $q$ be reduction modulo $6$. Since every residue modulo $6$ has a preimage modulo $12$, two maps out of $\T$ agreeing after precomposition with $q$ agree on every residue class.
\end{example}

For finite rings, one may build a preimage table for $q$, check equality of the two composites on all of $R$, and then infer equality on $\T$. Explicit enumeration costs $O(|R|+|\T|)$ ring-map evaluations.

\begin{example}[Two-variable residual specialization]
Let
\[
\Lambda=\mathbb Z[u,v],\qquad
\mathfrak m=(5,u-2,v-3).
\]
Evaluation at $(u,v)=(2,3)$ followed by reduction modulo $5$ identifies $\Lambda/\mathfrak m$ with $\mathbb F_5$. For example,
\[
u^2+uv+2v+1\longmapsto 4+6+6+1=17\equiv2\pmod5.
\]
The nonzero residue $2$ has inverse $3$.
\end{example}

\section{Conceptual interpretation}

For each coefficient algebra $A$, the set $\Hom_{\Lambda\text{-alg}}(R,A)$ is the set of $A$-valued points of the affine space represented by $R$, while $\Hom_{\Lambda\text{-alg}}(\T,A)$ is the analogous Hecke point set. The comparison identifies these sets for every $A$ and is compatible with every coefficient map $A\to B$. It therefore identifies full functors of points, not merely selected numerical solutions.

This distinction matters for families. A field-valued point records one specialization, whereas a map to the dual numbers $k[\varepsilon]/(\varepsilon^2)$ can record a first-order tangent direction. Because transport permits arbitrary coefficient algebras, infinitesimal information crosses the comparison together with ordinary points.

Freeness has a complementary geometric interpretation. If $R$ is finite free of rank $n$ over $\Lambda$, its structure morphism to weight space is finite flat of constant module-theoretic degree $n$. The Hecke presentation has exactly the same structure. Constant degree does not, however, prevent ramification or intersections of branches; those phenomena require differentials, tangent maps, or Selmer-theoretic obstruction groups.

The surjective uniqueness principle is likewise a coordinate statement. If every Hecke function comes from a deformation function, two Hecke points agreeing on all deformation coordinates agree on every function. Without surjectivity, unobserved coordinates can distinguish points.

\section{Stable-Yoshida applications}

Assume the arithmetic comparison identifies the universal minimal ordinary deformation ring of the stable-Yoshida residual representation with the localized genus-two ordinary Hecke algebra over a two-variable Iwasawa algebra.

The Abstract Minimal Modularity Lifting Theorem then says that every coefficient-valued minimal ordinary Galois deformation point arises from exactly one Hecke eigenpacket. Because coefficient rings are arbitrary, this includes classical points and infinitesimal deformations. The Freeness Transfer Theorem shows that freeness of the ordinary Hecke algebra over weight space is equivalent to freeness of the universal deformation ring, with rank preserved. The Surjective Eigenpacket Uniqueness Theorem isolates the algebraic cancellation behind uniqueness of an ordinary family. The Residual Weight Ring Theorem guarantees that maximal weight specialization has a field as its base.

These consequences do not establish the comparison. Its construction must address ordinary local deformation conditions, minimal ramification, symplectic polarization, residual irreducibility, Hecke-valued Galois representations, local--global compatibility, and a numerical or patching criterion. Likewise, finite freeness alone does not calculate rank or prove generic \'{e}taleness, and pointwise uniqueness alone does not describe singular intersections of geometric branches.

\section{Future directions}

An integral stable-Yoshida modularity lifting theorem should establish $R\cong\T$ before inverting $p$. The stable Yoshida description may reduce the residual four-dimensional problem to controlled two-dimensional data while preserving the required symplectic polarization.

A finite-flat strengthening should prove that the common ring is finite flat and generically \'{e}tale over the two-variable weight algebra, with rank equal to the multiplicity of the ordinary Hecke component. Freeness gives constant rank; generic \'{e}taleness should be related to vanishing of adjoint Selmer obstructions on a dense set of classical points.

Rigidity should also be investigated beyond very regular weights. The true boundary may be governed by the local degree and reducedness of the weight map, with failure concentrated on a congruence or endoscopic locus. At critical points where uniqueness fails, tangent directions of companion families should be detected by ordinary adjoint Selmer groups and organized by refinements of local crystalline representations.

\section{Conclusion}

The algebraic output of a minimal ordinary $R=\T$ theorem is a unified transport package. The comparison identifies deformation points with eigenpackets over every coefficient algebra and is natural under coefficient change. Surjectivity gives uniqueness by cancellation. Linear equivalence transfers freeness and rank. Maximal specialization produces a residual field.

For genus-two Siegel systems arising from stable Yoshida residual representations, this separation clarifies the proof architecture: deep arithmetic constructs the comparison, while universal algebra supplies modularity, uniqueness, freeness, and residual integrality. It also identifies the next questions---integrality, rank, ramification, and companion-family geometry---that require genuinely new arithmetic input.

